For increasing function, $\quad f(x)>0, \forall x\begin{array}{lr}\Rightarrow & f^{\prime}(x)=2 e^x+a e^{-x}+(2 a+1)>0 \\ \Rightarrow & \left(2 e^x+1\right)+a\left(e^{-x}+2\right)>0 \\ \Rightarrow & a\left(e^{-x}+2\right)>-\left(1+2 e^x\right) \\ \Rightarrow & a>\frac{-\left(1+2 e^x\right)}{\left(e^{-x}+2\right)}\end{array} \begin{array}{lrl}\Rightarrow$& a & >$\frac{-\left(1+2 e^x\right)}{\left(1+2 e^x\right)} \cdot$ e^x \\ \Rightarrow & a & >-e^x \\ \because & e^x \in(0, $\infty)$& \Rightarrow-e^x \in(-$\infty,0)\end{array}
and $ a>-e^x $\begin{array}{ll}\therefore$& a \in[0, \infty)\end{array}

Let $y=x^x\Rightarrow \frac{d y}{d x}=x^x(1+\log x)$<br/>For increaṣing function, $\frac{d y}{d x}>0\begin{array}{cc}
\Rightarrow & x^x(1+\log x)>0 \\
\Rightarrow & 1+\log x>0 \\
\Rightarrow & \log _e x>\log _e \frac{1}{e} \\
